A||N||11||Monkeyman||Wheel spacers?||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||12-08-1999||12:03 AM||||I got a good deal (free) on a set of 15" honeycomb rims from my brother-in-laws '90 Beretta GT.  Only prob is that the offset is wrong.  The offset seems to be about a 1/2" too much.  Anybody know what it would cost to add spacers and longer lug bolts?  Are there any disadvantages to this?  I'm looking for stock GT rims, but if I can do this much cheaper, I might stick with these.||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||12-08-1999||07:31 AM||||Somewhere there is an old topic where we discuss this. I didn't recommend it, but I did find a company that sells what looks like high quality spacers. Apparently, I didn't bookmark the site, so you may have to search for the old thread to find the URL. Years ago, my brother tried using some "el cheapo" aluminum spacers and they would loosen up if you even looked at them cross-eyed. The front wheel studs are fairly easy to replace, I haven't done the back. Wheel studs are pretty inexpensive as parts go.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||12-08-1999||12:09 PM||||Monkeyman... I'm surprised that 1/2" makes that much difference. Good info, though. I almost bought an identical set of those wheels, a while back. Where does it hit? What year is your car? TIA.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||12-08-1999||01:04 PM||||I have an '86SC.  It seems to hit the metal to the bottom and outside of the springs.  I don't know that it's a 1/2" exactly. I put a board on the tire and measured from there.  The tires seem to have about the same bulge in the sidewall, so it seems to be about a 1/2".  I don't know why this is a problem, either.  I haven't measured the rim width (tough to do with the tire mounted), but I doubt it's 8".  That would be the next logical width and I know that 7'ers would fit.  7" width was standard on '87 GTs (at least on the rear) and they didn't change the suspension til '88.  I can't figure it out.  Someone said something about a lip on the outside of the wheels and these seem to have that.  The only wheels I have to compare these to are the 14" sawblades.||||reg||1
Z||000004||dogtired||12-08-1999||01:21 PM||||Most spacers I have seen are JUNK?<BR>After you purchase the spacers and new lug bolts? It starts to get high priced  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Is it the tire or the wheel that is rubbing? Metal to metal or rubber to metal? Could it be just an extra wide profile tire? Just some thoughts! Enjoy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-08-1999||02:32 PM||||Unfortunatly, it's the wheel that's rubbing.  I just wish it was the tire.  It's the same size tire that's on everybody elses car (P205/60/15).  Thanx, anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||12-08-1999||08:11 PM||||I'm sure you would have seen it, or checked for it, but on some wheels, the dimensions are printed on the inside(towards the car) of the wheel. I know that mine say the rim width on them, don't know if the offset is printed there or not.  Mine are the factory wheels, not aftermarket.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||12-08-1999||09:52 PM||||I bet they'd fit if you installed rear coil-overs.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-08-1999||11:26 PM||||fiero56--I never knew that there were dimensions printed there...Just checked.  There isn't anything printed there.  These aren't aftermarket rims.  They are the stock rims for a '90 Beretta GT.<P>batboy--I'll probably install coil-overs sometime in the future, but I heard that coil-overs are pretty expensive.  Unless you have a set you want to donate;-)||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||12-09-1999||02:42 AM||||Actually, I was hoping you might have a set of Beretta wheels that you might be willing to donate to me...haha. I can't get any tires I want to fit my 14" wheels I have, so I'm thinking of getting a set of inexpensive 15" wheels. I've been debating whether or not to get rear coil overs too. Check out this link for coil-overs.<P> <A HREF="http://www.shelbycustom.com/r-coil/r-coil.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.shelbycustom.com/r-coil/r-coil.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||12-09-1999||04:03 AM||||The rear coil-overs aren't too bad for price, but the front ones are outrageous.  Next year I might go for the rear coil-overs,  but I bet my tires are gonna rub on the front, too.  (I was too frustrated to even try to put one on the front.)||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phaeton||01-02-2000||04:54 AM||||I'm fairly new and have been reading old threads (its -50 outside and I just don't want to go driving).  I use beretta GTZ 16" wheels.  I had to go with 1/2" spacers and longer studs in the rear and 1/4" spacers in the front (same studs).  The spacers were fabricated at a local machine shop at $40 each.  Using 225/50-16 on all four corners with the spacers cuts down the understeer some.  This is an 88GT and the spacers keep the tread centers at the factory settings.  Plus they are available in Bright Red with black center caps and nut covers-look damn fine on a black Fiero.  ||||reg||1
